---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { gapRuns, meta } from '../lib/data';
import { fmt } from '../lib/format';

const m = meta();
const { monthLabel, dateShort } = fmt('en');
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const gaps = gapRuns();
const P = m.placement;
const G = P.by_geocode;
const B = P.by_placement;
const num = (v: number) => v.toLocaleString('en-GB');
const share = (v: number | undefined) =>
  `${(((v ?? 0) / Math.max(1, P.total)) * 100).toFixed(0)}%`;
---

<Base
  lang="en"
  path="/method"
  title="Method, and what this cannot tell you — AL Pulse"
  active="areas"
  description="How the AL Pulse figures are produced, and the things they cannot answer."
>
  <h1>Method, and what this cannot tell you</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The useful question about a dashboard is not whether it has errors but
    whether anyone is looking for them. These are the ones found so far.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the numbers come from</h2>
  <p>
    Every figure derives from the Registo Nacional de Turismo's public register
    of Alojamento Local. The register is scraped month by month back to 2007,
    transformed with dbt into DuckDB, and rendered here at build time. Nothing
    on this site queries anything at read time.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two facts are recorded per registration: when it was registered, and whether
    it was still on the register the last time we looked. Everything else is
    derived from those.
  </p>

  <h2>The register has been pulled {m.pulls.length} times</h2>
  <p>
    This is the single most important caveat, and it shapes every chart of
    losses. A registration that has gone can only be detected by comparing two
    consecutive pulls — so the first month any loss is visible at all is
    <strong>{monthLabel(m.loss_observable_from)}</strong>, the month closed by
    the second pull. Before that the loss series is not zero; it is unknown, and
    the charts leave it blank rather than draw a flat line implying that nothing
    ever lapsed.
  </p>
  {
    gaps.length > 0 && (
      <p>
        There {gaps.length === 1 ? 'is also a gap' : `are also ${gaps.length} gaps`}.{' '}
        {gaps.map((g) => (
          <>
            No pull closed{' '}
            {g.months === 1 ? (
              <strong>{monthLabel(g.from)}</strong>
            ) : (
              <>
                <strong>{monthLabel(g.from)}</strong> to{' '}
                <strong>{monthLabel(g.to)}</strong> ({g.months} months)
              </>
            )},{' '}
            so every licence that lapsed then is attributed to{' '}
            {g.filedUnder ? monthLabel(g.filedUnder) : '—'}, the month closed by
            the first pull that found it missing.{' '}
          </>
        ))}
        The decline was gradual; the chart shows it as a cliff. Read the step
        as "this is what we found when we next looked", not as an event in that
        month.
      </p>
    )
  }
  <p class="small faint">
    Pulls so far: {m.pulls.map((p) => dateShort(p.date)).join(' · ')}. A pull
    in the first days of a month closes the month before it — the one on the
    2nd is there to see the previous month complete — and the losses it finds
    are filed under that earlier month. Each pull closes a month before the
    next pull's: a mid-month pull followed by an early one in the next month
    closes the month before its own, so that month keeps a bar of its own.
  </p>

  <h2>What a "total" counts</h2>
  <p>
    The running total is registrations dated on or before a given month that are
    still on the register, less those seen to have gone. Its shape is reliable;
    its level is exact only at a pull month. Where this site names a peak, it
    takes the peak over pull months only — a maximum inside an unobserved
    stretch would be an artefact of when we looked.
  </p>

  <h2>Two registers, and why only one has history</h2>
  <p>
    Tourism is a regional competence in the autonomous regions. Madeira reports
    into the national register; the Azores do not. Azorean operators register
    with the Direção Regional do Turismo and receive a regional number (RRAL),
    so the national export carries only
    {' '}{m.azores.in_national_register.toLocaleString('en-GB')} Azorean
    establishments against the
    {' '}{m.azores.listings.toLocaleString('en-GB')} on the regional register.
    This site was previously described as excluding the Azores because their
    data was not updated. That was the wrong diagnosis — the national register
    <em>is</em> updated for the Azores, it is simply the wrong register.
  </p>
  <p>
    The regional one is published as open data
    (<a href="https://dados.gov.pt/datasets/alojamento-local-em-funcionamento-na-regiao-autonoma-dos-acores-raa">
      Alojamento Local em funcionamento na Região Autónoma dos Açores</a>,
    CC-BY) and this site reads it directly, so
    {' '}{m.azores.localities.toLocaleString('en-GB')} Azorean localities and
    {' '}{m.azores.municipalities} municipalities now have real counts, pages
    and map polygons.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>It records no dates.</strong> There is no registration date column
    and no opening date column — not stale dates, no dates. Everything on this
    site that means <em>compared with the same thing at another time</em>
    therefore cannot exist for the Azores:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>no monthly series, so no chart on an Azorean area page;</li>
    <li>no growth figure over any period;</li>
    <li>
      no rank, because every rank here is computed inside the monthly series.
      Ranking an Azorean area against the national set for the current month
      would produce a number that could not be compared with the rank movement
      printed beside it, so the rank is shown as absent instead;
    </li>
    <li>
      and no losses: a lapsed licence is only visible by comparing two pulls,
      and the Azorean pulls start now.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    All of those read <span class="num">—</span> or <span class="num">n/a</span>
    rather than zero. Zero is a measurement; this is an absence. Each monthly
    pull is kept, so an Azorean series will accumulate from
    {' '}{monthLabel(m.data_through)} forward — it just cannot be back-filled.
  </p>
  <p>
    One consequence worth naming: the national headline figure on the overview
    page excludes the Azores, and the Azorean total is shown beside it rather
    than added into it. A single number mixing a fourteen-year series with a
    one-month snapshot would disagree with every chart underneath it.
  </p>

  <h3>How Azorean records are placed on the map</h3>
  <p>
    The regional register gives each establishment a concelho, a freguesia and
    a coordinate. The names do not match OpenStreetMap's: they disagree about
    the freguesia for 47% of records, mostly over parenthetical qualifiers and
    saints' names, so matching on names alone would place barely half of them.
    Each record is therefore located by its coordinate, against the same
    freguesia polygons the rest of the site uses —
    {' '}99.4% land inside one. The remainder fall back to name matching and
    then to the concelho alone; a record that resolves to no area is counted
    nowhere and reported. Where a coordinate lands on a different island from
    the one the register states, the coordinate is rejected rather than
    believed.
  </p>
  <p>
    The published register also carries the operator's name, e-mail and phone
    number in every row. Those columns are dropped before anything is written
    to disk, and a test fails the build if they reappear.
  </p>

  <h2>How national registrations are placed</h2>
  <p>
    The national register gives an address and a postcode but no coordinate.
    Each registration is geocoded against INE's
    {' '}<a href="https://dados.gov.pt/en/datasets/base-nacional-de-moradas/">Base Nacional de Moradas</a>,
    about six million address points, and the rule that produced the point is
    kept with it. Of the {num(P.total)} registrations on the register now:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>{share(G.address)} matched to the door — same postcode, street and number;</li>
    <li>{share((G.street ?? 0) + (G.street_cp4 ?? 0))} matched to the street, without the number;</li>
    <li>{share(G.postcode)} only to the postcode, at the middle of its addresses;</li>
    <li>{share((G.postcode_area ?? 0) + (G.none ?? 0))} to a whole postcode area, or not at all.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A registration is put in a freguesia by its point when that point is good
    to about a kilometre or better and lies in the municipality the register
    names, and otherwise by the locality the register names. INE's file has a
    small share of misplaced points — the same door stored twice, kilometres
    apart — so points far from the rest of their postcode are discarded first. The point comes first because the register's own freguesia names are
    often the pre-2013 ones or abbreviated past matching. INE's addresses were
    last updated in 2019, so streets built since then fall back to the postcode.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here the points are used only to choose an area: every chart, table and
    map on these pages is a count for an area. Nothing on this site says who
    runs a registration — the register's operator details are never published.
  </p>

  <h2>What is excluded</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Nothing, for want of a place.</strong> A registration that can be
      matched only to its municipality — {num(B.municipality ?? 0)} now — counts
      there and nationally but in no freguesia; one that matches nothing —
      {' '}{num(B.unplaced ?? 0)} — counts nationally only.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Personal data the source withdrew</h2>
  <p>
    Until 2026-09 the public export carried the operator's name, tax number and
    contact details. It no longer does — ten columns went. Nothing on this site
    ever used them, and historical values are not shown.
  </p>

  <h2>Known open issues</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Registrations on streets newer than INE's 2019 addresses are placed by
      postcode, which in the countryside can be kilometres wide.
    </li>
    <li>
      Losses in an unobserved month are attributed to the next pull, as above.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Reading the charts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Every mark answers at the pointer — hover, tap or focus and use the arrow keys.</li>
    <li>A change smaller than half a percentage point is rendered "no change" rather than a direction.</li>
    <li>Bars start at zero; where an axis is truncated, it is labelled.</li>
    <li>Colour never carries meaning alone — every series is also named.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Data through {monthLabel(m.data_through)}; site built {m.generated}. The
    pipeline and this site are open source.
    <a href={`${base}/`}>Back to the overview</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
